.TH GPIO-WATCH 8 2026-09-21 "Linux 7.0.9" "System Manager's Manual"
.SH NAME
gpio\-watch \- report changes to the state of GPIO lines
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B \%gpio\-watch
.I chip
.I offset
.RI [ offset ]...
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B \%gpio\-watch
watches lines of a GPIO chip for changes to their line information,
using the version 2 GPIO character device interface,
and prints a line for each change the kernel reports.
It does not request the lines itself;
it reports what other consumers do with them.
.PP
.I chip
is the path of a GPIO character device, such as
.IR /dev/gpiochip0 .
Each
.I offset
names a line on that chip and is converted with
.BR strtoul (3)
in base 0, so it may be given in decimal, in hexadecimal with a leading
.BR 0x ,
or in octal with a leading
.BR 0 .
.PP
For each change the program prints:
.PP
.in +4n
.EX
line \fIoffset\fP: \fIevent\fP at \fItimestamp\fP
.EE
.in
.PP
where
.I event
is one of:
.TP
.B requested
The line was requested by a consumer.
.TP
.B released
The line was released by its consumer.
.TP
.B config changed
The line was reconfigured.
.PP
.I timestamp
is the time of the change in nanoseconds, as recorded by the kernel from
.BR CLOCK_MONOTONIC .
.PP
The program runs until it is killed or an error occurs.
.SH OPTIONS
.B \%gpio\-watch
takes no options.
If fewer than two arguments are given, or an
.I offset
contains characters that are not part of a number, it prints a usage
message to standard output and exits.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.B \%gpio\-watch
exits with status 1 on a usage error or any other error, including an
event type it does not recognise.
It does not otherwise exit.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /dev/gpiochipN
GPIO character device given as
.IR chip ,
opened for reading and writing.
.SH NOTES
The kernel must provide the GPIO character device
.RB \%( CONFIG_GPIO_CDEV ).
The caller needs read and write access to the device node.
.PP
Each line can be watched only once per open of the device;
giving the same
.I offset
twice makes the kernel refuse the second watch with
.BR EBUSY ,
and the program exits.
.PP
The kernel buffers at most 32 change events per open of the device.
If more occur before they are read, the most recent are discarded,
and this cannot be detected from the output.
.SH EXAMPLES
Watch lines 3 and 4 of the first GPIO chip:
.PP
.in +4n
.EX
gpio\-watch /dev/gpiochip0 3 4
.EE
.in
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR \%gpio\-event\-mon (8),
.BR \%gpio\-sloppy\-logic\-analyzer (8)
.PP
The GPIO character device interface and line information watches are
described in the Linux kernel source in
.PP
.I \%Documentation/userspace\-api/gpio/chardev.rst
.br
.I \%Documentation/userspace\-api/gpio/gpio\-v2\-get\-lineinfo\-watch\-ioctl.rst
